    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the course was planned by AI agents from research notes on copyright and the public domain, the three definitions of open, the permissive and copyleft software licenses, Creative Commons, SPDX, REUSE and license compatibility, read from the license texts, their authors' own pages and the specifications, each fetched with an honest User-Agent after a check of the site's robots.txt; an outline was drafted from the notes and reviewed, and the chapters were then written from it in English and translated from the English into Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French. The working notes, the outline and the glossaries are kept with the course's sources, not in the library."@en ,
                 "The course was checked by machine (the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators, its course and Markdown checks, a second SHACL validator, and the course's draft checker, which compares every translation with the English) and by AI agents against the sources; it has had no human review."@en ,
                 "Selection: what someone who publishes code, data or content needs to read a license and apply one: why a work is all rights reserved by default, the public domain and CC0, and the Open Source Definition, the Free Software Definition and the Open Definition; the MIT License, the two BSD licenses and Apache-2.0; copyleft in GPL-2.0, GPL-3.0, AGPL-3.0, LGPL-3.0, MPL-2.0 and EUPL-1.2, and \"only\" against \"or later\"; the four Creative Commons elements and six licenses, attribution and ShareAlike, and why CC licenses are not meant for code; SPDX 3.0.1 identifiers and license expressions, and REUSE 3.3 headers, the LICENSES folder and REUSE.toml; and compatibility only where the license authors state it themselves: the GPL versions, Apache-2.0 and the GPL, MPL-2.0 and the GNU licenses, CC BY-SA 4.0 and GPL-3.0, and the EUPL-1.2 appendix. The course says at its start that it is not legal advice. Left out on purpose: questions on which license to choose, which have more than one defensible answer; the term of copyright in years and other rules that differ by country; the version and size of the SPDX License List, which change with each release; the ASF's third-party policy as a question, since it is one project's rule and not a ruling on compatibility; and the Standard for Public Code's license criterion beyond one sentence, since another course in the library covers it."@en ,
                 "Structure: 6 chapters of 27 steps in all. Each step is a short theory text in Markdown, read in two or three chunks, and two or three multiple-choice questions on it; the SPDX and REUSE chapter adds short license expressions, a file header and a REUSE.toml example. Each chapter has review questions that combine its steps and are asked only in its final review (two to four per chapter): 59 step questions and 16 review questions, 75 questions in all. Every question has one correct option (the card's back) and three wrong options (its distractors) of similar length and form, each with a note on why it is wrong. Every text is in English, Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French; license names, SPDX identifiers and the code examples are the same in every language."@en ,
                 "Licensing: the course is CC0 1.0, like most of the library. Its theory, questions, options, notes and examples are in the course's own words. The Creative Commons legal code, the Standard for Public Code and Wikidata's data are dedicated to the public domain. The pages under CC BY 4.0 (the Open Source Definition, the Open Definition and Creative Commons' FAQ, wiki and site pages) were used for facts restated in the course's own words, with a few short phrases quoted and attributed, and they are credited in the sources below. The REUSE Specification is CC BY-SA 4.0: its facts are restated and nothing is copied from it. The gnu.org pages are CC BY-ND 4.0 and allow no translation, so the course takes only facts from them and quotes nothing. The GNU license texts allow only verbatim copies, and the course quotes no more of them than the words \"or any later version\" and the short notice that carries them; the MPL-2.0, EUPL-1.2, Apache-2.0, MIT and BSD texts were used for facts, with a few words quoted and attributed. The WIPO and WTO pages show no license, the MPL FAQ is CC BY-SA 3.0 or later and the ASF's pages on compatibility and its third-party policy are under Apache-2.0: all were used for facts only, with nothing copied. The SPDX License List says \"All Rights Reserved\" and the SPDX annex names no single license: facts, identifiers and syntax only. No Creative Commons logos or buttons are used."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the whole release, with the library checker (npm run library:check), which validates it against Solid Memo's shapes (library deck format 6, card format 5, chapter, step and distractor format 1), DCAT-AP and SKOS, with the reference data, checks the course by its rules and its Markdown by the rules for a release, and with a second, independent SHACL validator (pySHACL, npm run crosscheck) over the same shapes; and every chapter with the course's draft checker, for its structure and for the same steps, questions, options, Markdown flags and theory chunks in every language, with code spans and code blocks byte for byte the same as in the English."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the release conforms to every shape, profile and rule the checker applies to it, and the crosscheck agrees."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Outline: facts and question design (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the outline checked by two AI agents, one that tried to refute every teaching point, answer and wrong option against the research notes and the primary texts, and one for the questions' design (one answer, recall from the front alone, options of the same form), for the size of each step and for overlap with the library's Standard for Public Code course; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 50 findings (18 on facts, 32 on design): 45 fixed in the outline or, for one, when the chapters were written, 1 fixed in part, 1 rejected with a reason and 3 checked with no change needed; among them a front with more than one correct answer from Creative Commons' own reasons, an Open Definition question that integrity also answered, a NoDerivatives rule misstated, a wrong option that the license's own words allowed, a question on one project's policy dropped, and backs that stood out by form."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Chapter text in English: facts, design and language (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: each chapter as written, reviewed by two AI agents: one for every fact, quotation, answer and wrong option against the research notes and the primary texts, fetched again where needed; one for the questions' design, each front read alone first, for wording, for terms used before they are taught and for the Markdown; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 91 findings across the six chapters (50 on facts, 41 on design and language), all fixed in the text but 1, a check that found the text correct; among them a claim that every CC license includes BY, which the CC 1.0 licenses do not, the FSF's view of NoDerivatives overstated, a wrong option that could be defended from another FSF statement, the OSD's free-redistribution criterion stated without its qualifier, wording too close to texts that may not be adapted, and backs and options that differed in form."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Course as a whole in English (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the six chapters read together in order by an AI agent, for facts taught twice, contradictions, cross-references, the way license names and identifiers are written, and every question's options (backs that are the longest or stand out by form, and fronts that do not work as flip cards); not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 9 findings, all fixed (one in part); among them one rule for writing license names against SPDX identifiers, two review questions that repeated a step's question, backs that were the longest option in 33 of the 75 questions, shortened where they stood out, chapter 6 facts that now point back to chapter 3, and a point attributed to the FSF that chapter 4 had given as Creative Commons' own."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-31T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Translations into Swedish, Korean, German, Spanish and French (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: for each language, a glossary of the course's terms drawn up first by an AI agent, from the official translations of the Creative Commons legal code and deeds, the EUPL-1.2 in that language, the GNU project's translations and the Open Definition's where they exist; each chapter translated from the English by an AI agent, then reviewed for fidelity, by translating it back into English and comparing it with the English, and as a native editor for natural language, terminology and typography; then each language's six chapters read together by an AI agent for consistency; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: 384 findings across the 30 chapter translations and the five consistency passes (Swedish 91, Korean 63, German 86, Spanish 85, French 59), 341 of them in the chapter reviews and 43 in the consistency passes, all fixed in the text; one German back was left longer than its wrong options, because the official German name of the Open Definition condition it gives sets its length."@en .

# The sources (W3C PROV-O): what the course took from each, and on what license.
<https://www.wipo.int/treaties/en/ip/berne/summary_berne.html>
